From 98e3dfe3c7ab1f3718f20dcd3510a560f52b1ba2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: C0fuge <189659128+C0fuge@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 03:59:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] libhdfs3: SASL data-transfer protection on the read path
 (dfs.data.transfer.protection)

A DataNode on a non-privileged port with dfs.data.transfer.protection
requires the SASL handshake of Hadoop's SaslDataTransferClient before
any op; libhdfs3 sent READ_BLOCK in plain and the DataNode dropped it.

- SessionConfig: dfs.data.transfer.protection (authentication,
  integrity, privacy; comma list in preference order) -> DIGEST-MD5
  QOPs auth, auth-int, auth-conf
- RemoteBlockReader: on a new connection, when protection is set, the
  block token is non-empty and the transfer port is >= 1024 (as
  Hadoop: privileged ports are trusted), DataTransferSaslConnect does
  the handshake: magic 0xDEADBEEF, varint-delimited
  DataTransferEncryptorMessageProto, DIGEST-MD5 (patch 0003) with user
  base64(token identifier), password base64(token password),
  digest-uri hdfs/0; ERROR / ERROR_UNKNOWN_KEY -> HdfsIOException, so
  the stream tries the next replica
- auth-int / auth-conf: SaslSocket decorates the socket: every write is
  wrapped into frames of int32 length + token (chunks of the server's
  maxbuf), every read unwraps one frame (SaslInputStream /
  SaslOutputStream); pooled connections keep their SaslSocket (the
  DataNode keeps the SASL streams for the connection's later ops)
- short-circuit reads and the write Pipeline unchanged; no AES cipher
  option (the DataNode then uses DIGEST-MD5 auth-conf itself)
